answer:
Use const by default. Reach for let only when you genuinely need to reassign the variable later.

code:
let retries = 3;
retries = 5;
const appName = 'Test Runner';
console.log(retries, appName, typeof retries);

real example:
A Playwright config object holds baseURL and timeout. Those never change, so they are const. A reviewer seeing let there will ask why.

It throws a TypeError and the old value is kept. In sloppy mode the assignment is silently ignored instead of throwing, which is even worse because the bug hides.

code:
const appName = 'Test Runner';
try {
  appName = 'Other Runner';
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.constructor.name);
}
console.log(appName);

real example:
If a helper does const result = 'pass' and then tries result = 'fail', the test silently reports the wrong status. Use let instead of const there.

var is function scoped, so it ignores blocks. let is block scoped, so it exists only inside the nearest pair of braces.

code:
function demo() {
  if (true) {
    var leaky = 1;
    let tight = 2;
    console.log('inside block:', leaky, tight);
  }
  console.log('after block, var:', leaky);
}
demo();

real example:
A var declared inside an if block leaks into the whole function and can collide with a same-named variable in another block. That collision is a bug that shows up only for some inputs.

A block is any pair of braces. A let declared inside it dies with the block, and touching it afterwards is a ReferenceError.

code:
let total = 1;
{
  let total = 2;
  console.log('inside the block:', total);
}
console.log('outside the block:', total);
try {
  console.log(totals);
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name);
}

real example:
Scraping data often needs a scratch variable inside a for loop. Declare it with let so it cannot leak into the rest of the test file.

You can, but in sloppy mode the assignment quietly creates a global on the global object. In strict mode it is a ReferenceError instead.

code:
total = 5;
console.log(typeof total);
console.log('it is now a global:', globalThis.total);
delete globalThis.total;
console.log('after delete:', typeof globalThis.total);

real example:
One test file forgets the keyword. The global leaks into a second test file that runs later in the same process, and now the two files interfere with each other.

A name can contain letters, digits, underscore and dollar sign. It cannot start with a digit, it cannot be a reserved word, and it is case sensitive.

code:
const _userId = 7;
const $button = 'submit';
const user$name = 'Ana';
// const 2fast = 'no';   // uncommenting this line breaks the entire file
console.log(_userId, $button, user$name);

real example:
Naming a const $button is normal in DOM code because of the old jQuery convention. In a test file prefer plain names like submitButton so the intent is obvious.

Yes. userName and username are two completely different variables, and a typo in case gives you undefined rather than an error.

code:
const userName = 'Ana';
const username = 'Bob';
console.log(userName, username);
console.log('same variable?', userName === username);

real example:
Reading a value off a page object with the wrong capitalisation gives undefined, and the failure message points at the wrong line entirely.

It is a SyntaxError, so the whole file never runs. That is deliberate: a const that is never initialised would be useless. Use let when you need a placeholder.

code:
const total = 0;
// const subtotal;   // uncommenting this line breaks the entire file
let subtotal;
subtotal = 12;
console.log(total, subtotal, typeof subtotal);

real example:
A test author writes const expected; and the runner reports a parse error for the file, not for that line. The real problem is much earlier in the stack.

Declaring creates the box. Assigning puts a value in it. A let can be declared empty, a const cannot.

code:
let result;
console.log(result, typeof result);
result = 'flaky';
console.log(result);
const ready = true;
console.log(ready, typeof ready);

real example:
A let counter declared at the top of a spec and filled in by each step is a common and readable pattern. The cost is that its type is not obvious from the declaration.

undefined. It is the default value of every let and var that has no initial value, and typeof reports it as undefined.

code:
let clickCount;
console.log('value:', clickCount);
console.log('typeof:', typeof clickCount);
clickCount = 0;
console.log('after assignment:', clickCount);

real example:
A beforeEach hook usually does clickCount = 0 to clear state. Forgetting that line lets counts leak from one test into the next.

No. const only stops you pointing the variable at a different value. The object it holds can still be changed.

code:
const user = { name: 'Ana' };
user.name = 'Bob';
user.role = 'admin';
console.log(user);
try {
  user = { name: 'Cid' };
} catch (e) {
  console.log('reassigning the box:', e.name);
}

real example:
Team code often says const in a comment and implies deep immutability. A test that mutates that object anyway passes, and the shared fixture is left dirty for the next test.

Because const locks the reference, not the contents. Object.freeze is what stops property changes, and it only works one level deep unless you freeze nested objects too.

code:
const config = { retries: 2 };
config.retries = 5;
console.log('const only:', config.retries);
const frozen = Object.freeze({ retries: 2 });
try {
  frozen.retries = 9;
} catch (e) {
  console.log('freeze can throw:', e.name);
}
console.log('after trying to write:', frozen.retries);

real example:
Shared test fixtures are usually frozen. If you freeze a top-level object but leave nested objects open, one test can still corrupt what the next test reads.

The binding is the variable name and the box it points at. Changing the value means editing what is inside. const locks the binding only, so contents stay editable.

code:
const list = [1, 2];
list.push(3);
console.log(list, list.length);
const alias = list;
alias.push(4);
console.log('two names, one array:', list);

real example:
Passing a list into a helper that pushes to it changes the caller's list too. That aliasing bug is the reason to copy with slice or spread before mutating.

The right hand side reads x before the let on the left has finished initialising. That window is the temporal dead zone, and reading the name in it is a ReferenceError.

code:
try {
  eval('let dup = dup;');
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name + ': ' + e.message);
}
console.log('outside the eval scope:', typeof dup);

real example:
This pattern shows up in minified bundles and in code written by developers used to other languages. The error names the dead zone, which is the clue to look one line up.

var is hoisted and initialised to undefined at the top of its scope, so the right hand side is a valid undefined. let exists but is locked until its declaration finishes, and reading it early throws.

code:
console.log(typeof hoisted);
var hoisted = hoisted;
console.log(hoisted, typeof hoisted);

real example:
Old jQuery plugin code relies on the var behaviour to test whether a global exists. Porting it to let turns a silent undefined into a hard crash.

It is the stretch of code between entering a scope and the line where a let or const actually gets its value. During that stretch the name exists but must not be read.

code:
function demo() {
  try {
    console.log('reading later:', later);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('blocked by the dead zone:', e.name);
  }
  let later = 5;
  console.log('after the declaration:', later);
}
demo();

real example:
The zone is per scope, not per line count. A function body that touches a parameter-like variable declared further down fails on the first test that reaches that path.

Hoisting moves the declaration up and gives it a value of undefined. The dead zone moves nothing: the binding stays put and any read before the declaration throws.

code:
function compare() {
  try {
    console.log(later);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('let says:', e.name);
  }
  let later = 2;
}
compare();
console.log('typeof is safe for var:', typeof maybeLater);
var maybeLater = 1;

real example:
That is why typeof is the safe way to feature detect an undeclared name, but typeof on a let variable inside the dead zone still throws.

typeof is only safe for names that were never declared. If a binding exists but has no value yet, typeof has to evaluate it, and that read throws.

code:
function check() {
  const results = [];
  try {
    results.push(typeof later);
  } catch (e) {
    results.push('typeof threw ' + e.name);
  }
  let later = 1;
  results.push(typeof later);
  return results;
}
console.log(check());
console.log('never declared is fine:', typeof notHereAnywhere);

real example:
Guard clauses that use typeof to validate input break when the input arrives as a let declared later in the same function. Guard against undefined with a default parameter instead.

Only by trying to read the value. Reading an undeclared name throws, reading a declared empty one gives undefined.

code:
let declared;
console.log(typeof declared, typeof neverDeclared);
try {
  console.log(neverDeclared);
} catch (e) {
  console.log('reading it throws:', e.name);
}
console.log('reading the declared one is fine:', declared);

real example:
Reporting code that logs typeof for every variable can hide typos. If a field must exist, assert on its value, not on its typeof.

It survives the block and stays alive for the rest of the function. If you declare it again elsewhere in the same function, the second declaration overwrites the first.

code:
if (true) {
  var status = 'pass';
  var status = 'fail';
}
console.log('after the block:', status);

real example:
Two loops in the same spec both declaring var count will corrupt each other. The failure shows up only for the data set that exercises both loops.

A var loop has one single binding for the whole loop. Every closure created inside it captures that same binding, so by the time you call them all they see the final value.

code:
const read = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  read.push(() => i);
}
console.log(read.map((fn) => fn()));
console.log('and i leaked out:', i);

real example:
A helper that registers a per-row callback in a for loop ends up reporting the last row for every row. It looks like flaky data, not like a scoping bug.

let in a for loop creates a fresh binding on every turn of the loop. Each closure captures its own copy, so each one remembers its own number.

code:
const read = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  read.push(() => i);
}
console.log(read.map((fn) => fn()));

real example:
Table-driven tests that queue one callback per row get the right row value per callback with let. Swapping that single keyword fixes a class of hard to reproduce failures.

Yes, with for...of and for...in the loop variable is a new const binding each turn, so it is the safest option. The classic counter form needs let, because const would block the increment.

code:
const visited = [];
for (const name of ['login', 'search', 'logout']) {
  visited.push(name.toUpperCase());
}
console.log(visited);
const keys = [];
for (const key in { a: 1, b: 2 }) {
  keys.push(key);
}
console.log(keys);

real example:
Iterating over a list of test case names with for...of and const means a callback inside the loop cannot accidentally write to the loop variable.

Shadowing is declaring a name inside a scope where an outer one already exists. The inner one hides the outer one for the rest of that scope, which is fine for constants but dangerous for configuration values.

code:
const timeout = 5000;
function run(timeout) {
  return 'uses the argument, not the outer value: ' + timeout;
}
console.log(run(30));
console.log('outer value is untouched:', timeout);

real example:
A function parameter named config hides the imported config, so the helper quietly uses the argument instead. ESLint no-shadow catches this before review does.

Because var carries three separate hazards: function scope instead of block scope, no temporal dead zone, and silent redeclaration. All three produce bugs that are hard to trace.

code:
var name = 'first';
var name = 'second';
console.log('var allows this:', name);
try {
  eval('let title = 1; let title = 2;');
} catch (e) {
  console.log('let does not:', e.name);
}

real example:
A codebase that bans var usually also enables prefer-const, which turns every accidental second assignment into a visible lint error.

Change the keyword to let and keep the name. If the variable is a parameter, nothing needs to change at all, because parameters are already mutable bindings.

code:
function grade(result) {
  let status = 'unknown';
  if (result > 90) status = 'pass';
  else if (result > 50) status = 'borderline';
  else status = 'fail';
  return status;
}
console.log(grade(95), grade(60), grade(10));

real example:
Reach for let only when a real reassignment exists. If a linter complains about an unused let, that is a signal the branch can be a ternary instead.

The assignment still works in sloppy mode but creates a global instead of a local variable. In strict mode, which most test runners use, it throws a ReferenceError.

code:
function helper() {
  retries = 3;
  return retries;
}
console.log('returned:', helper());
console.log('but it is global now:', globalThis.retries);
delete globalThis.retries;
console.log('after delete:', typeof globalThis.retries);

real example:
That leaked global makes a later assertion pass or fail depending on test order. Tests that only fail when the whole suite runs together are almost always leaking something like this.

In C a const value is baked into the binary. In JavaScript const is a runtime promise about one binding, and it says nothing about the object behind it.

code:
const LIMIT = 10;
const config = { limit: LIMIT };
config.limit = 99;
console.log('the variable:', LIMIT);
console.log('the object read it once:', config.limit);

real example:
An environment object built as const from process.env can still be mutated later by a helper, so two tests disagree about the base URL. Freeze it if it must stay put.

Nothing changes inside the function, because the mutation happens to the object, not to the binding. The difference only shows up if the function reassigns the parameter.

code:
function addTag(person, tag) {
  person.tags.push(tag);
}
const user = { name: 'Ana', tags: [] };
addTag(user, 'admin');
console.log(user);
console.log('the caller sees the change too:', user.tags.length);

real example:
A fixture passed into several helpers accumulates tags from all of them. Create the fixture inside each test, or copy it with spread, so each test starts clean.

The same TypeError. Destructuring with const creates const bindings, so each extracted name is locked.

code:
const { name, retries } = { name: 'Bo', retries: 2 };
console.log(name, retries);
try {
  name = 'Cy';
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name);
}
console.log('original kept:', name);

real example:
Pulling a value out of a config object with const and then trying to override it for one scenario is a frequent review comment. Build a new object with spread instead.

Use array destructuring assignment. Both bindings have to be let or var, because the swap reassigns them.

code:
let first = 'page-1.png';
let second = 'page-2.png';
[first, second] = [second, first];
console.log(first, second);
let x = 1;
let y = 2;
[x, y] = [y, x];
console.log(x, y);

real example:
Swapping two expected and actual values in a diff helper reads well this way. Note that the comma form on the right is a separate trick and is much less readable.

The parameter shadows the outer variable inside the function. If you mean the outer one you have to rename one of them, because the name now means the parameter.

code:
let retries = 3;
function runOnce(retryCount = retries) {
  return 'attempts: ' + retryCount;
}
console.log(runOnce());
console.log(runOnce(1));

real example:
A default parameter reading a module level constant is fine, but the moment a caller passes a value the constant is silently ignored. Give the default its own name to make that visible.

Object.freeze only freezes one level, so recurse into it. With freeze in place, writes fail silently in sloppy mode and throw in strict mode.

code:
function deepFreeze(value) {
  Object.values(value).forEach((child) => {
    if (child && typeof child === 'object') deepFreeze(child);
  });
  return Object.freeze(value);
}
const config = deepFreeze({ retries: 2, network: { timeout: 1000 } });
config.network.timeout = 5;
console.log('still the frozen value:', config.network.timeout);

real example:
Freezing a shared settings object stops one spec from leaking a changed timeout into the next. Do it once when the object is created, not inside each test.

Write const first. If the assignment fails with a TypeError, that is the compiler telling you the value really does change, so switch to let at that point.

code:
const ids = new Set();
ids.add('t-1');
ids.add('t-2');
console.log(ids.size, typeof ids);
const run = () => ids;
console.log('still the same set:', run().size);
// ids = new Set();   // uncommenting this throws a TypeError

real example:
Adopting const-by-default makes accidental reassignment loud instead of silent. Teams that roll this out usually pair it with a lint rule so it never regresses.

Think of a variable as a labelled box. const locks the label to that particular box. You cannot swap in a different box, but you can edit what is inside the one you have.

code:
const settings = { theme: 'dark', retries: 2 };
settings.theme = 'light';
console.log(settings);
try {
  settings = { theme: 'sepia', retries: 9 };
} catch (e) {
  console.log('swapping the box:', e.name);
}
console.log('the box is unchanged:', settings.theme);

real example:
This is why a const array built from push still has a fixed length check problem. Copy first if the count matters, since the reference never changes even when the contents do.
